Verdict: MAJOR  
Retrieval adequacy: insufficient

Findings:
- [major] The plan claims ecosystem coverage without ecosystem retrieval. In `Resource Intelligence Summary` and `Documents consulted`, every concrete repo surface cited is from `workspace-hub`; there is no tier-1 repo evidence even though the plan later promises “ecosystem inventory, gap analysis, and readiness recommendations across workspace-hub and tier-1 repos” in both `Acceptance Criteria` and the main package artifact. That is not a reviewable basis for a repo-ecosystem contract.
- [major] Hermes is explicitly in scope but missing from the planned discoverability/update surfaces. The issue scope requires “`CLAUDE.md`, `CODEX.md`, `GEMINI.md`, Hermes equivalents”, but `Contract-boundary rule`, `Artifact Map`, and `Files to Change` only name `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.codex/CODEX.md`, `.gemini/GEMINI.md`, and `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`. This omission is not theoretical: `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template`, `config/agents/hermes/SOUL.md`, and issue `#1583` show Hermes already has repo-managed surfaces that should be part of the contract.
- [major] `docs/reports/2026-04-20-issue-2399-provider-version-baseline.md` is underspecified to the point of likely fabrication. The plan says it will “record provider/version baseline” but names no authoritative source for installed CLI/provider versions, machine-local overrides, or cloud-run versions. The retrieved repo surfaces (`config/agents/codex/config.toml`, `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template`, `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh`) expose defaults and sync logic, not actual deployed versions. Without a source-of-truth acquisition method, this artifact will be stale or invented.
- [major] The eval battery is not yet defined in a way that supports the issue’s comparison goal. The issue asks for “golden tasks for comparing model releases before broad rollout,” but the plan only commits to `config/ai/model-release-smoke-battery.yaml`, fixtures, and a runner contract. Nowhere in `Pseudocode`, `TDD Test List`, or `Acceptance Criteria` is there a scoring rubric, normalization policy, or pass/fail decision contract for comparing different provider outputs on planning/review/handoff tasks. Expected-outcome fixtures alone do not make cross-provider outputs comparable.
- [major] Follow-up issue creation is approval-blocking but not operationally specified. `Pseudocode` says “for each highest-risk gap above threshold: create a follow-up GitHub issue via gh issue create,” and `Acceptance Criteria` requires “actual follow-up GitHub issues, not only drafts,” but the plan never defines the threshold artifact, dedupe/idempotency rules, required labels/owner, or failure handling if issue creation is unavailable. This is likely to create duplicate or inconsistent follow-on issues on reruns.
- [minor] The plan is not approval-ready on its own stated gate. `Adversarial Review Summary` is still entirely `PENDING`, yet the issue-planning workflow in [docs/plans/README.md](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/docs/plans/README.md) requires adversarial review artifacts before posting for approval. That is expected at this stage, but it still means the current draft is not ready to approve.

Missing tests:
- A test that the provider/version baseline is derived from named authoritative sources rather than repo defaults alone.
- A test that the smoke battery defines scoring/normalization semantics per task, not just fixture presence.
- A test that follow-up issue creation is idempotent and deduplicates against existing open issues for the same gap.
- A test that Hermes discoverability anchors exist wherever the contract requires provider entry points.

Scope creep concerns:
- The plan says it will not implement the future smoke runner, but it also adds a runner contract, a fixture corpus, baseline inventory, and automated follow-up issue creation. That is already drifting from “define the contract” into partial workflow design without enough operational detail to make those pieces safe.

Weakest assumption:
- That a workspace-hub-local document audit is sufficient to define a repo-ecosystem readiness contract spanning tier-1 repos, multi-machine Hermes/provider behavior, and version-upgrade decisions.

Most likely implementation failure mode:
- The resulting contract will look complete on paper but encode workspace-hub assumptions only, leaving tier-1 repo adapters, Hermes-specific surfaces, and real machine/version drift unmodeled.

Most likely test gap:
- Tests will prove files/sections exist, but not that the battery can actually compare two model releases in a reproducible, scored way.

Future issues suggested:
- Add a precursor issue or explicit plan section for authoritative provider/version inventory collection across local, synced, and cloud-run environments.
- Add a follow-on issue for cross-repo sampling/inventory of tier-1 adapter surfaces if this issue remains scoped as “repo ecosystem” rather than “workspace-hub control plane”.

Review confidence: high
